# In a gitattributes file the LAST matching pattern wins, so the general rule goes first and
# the exception after it. Getting this backwards silently does nothing, which is how the first
# version of this file was written.

# Normalise to LF in the repository, native endings in the working tree.
* text=auto

# Test fixtures are byte-exact inputs, not text that may be reformatted.
#
# `tests/test_transitions.py` asserts the sha256 of every ban-list fixture against the hashes
# recorded in `tests/fixtures/transitions/SOURCES.md`, because a selected fixture without
# provenance is data with no origin. If git rewrites the line endings on checkout, the bytes
# change and those hashes stop matching.
#
# Proven on 7 Nov 2026, with `core.autocrlf=true` and no attributes file: a fresh clone turned
# an 83-byte ban list into 87 bytes, and the checksum test failed in the clone while passing in
# the original working tree and on Linux CI. That is the worst shape of bug - green everywhere
# it is looked at, broken where it is used.
#
# `-text` rather than `binary`: no end-of-line conversion, but keep diffs, since these files
# are small and readable and a diff on one is exactly what a reviewer wants to see.
tests/fixtures/** -text
